-- 524 (3) "The judge or justice who hears the matter shall cancel a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order in respect of the accused if the judge or justice finds that (a) the accused has
-- contravened or had been about to contravene the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order; or (b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an offence while being
-- subject to the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order."
-- src:11696 | (3) The judge or justice who hears the matter shall cancel a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order in respect of the accused if the judge or justice finds that
-- src:11697 | (a) the accused has contravened or had been about to contravene the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order; or
-- src:11698 | (b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an offence while being subject to the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order.
@ref s 524(3) (src:11696-11698)
GIVEN `the judge or justice finds that the accused has contravened or had been about to contravene the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the judge or justice finds that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an offence while being subject to the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 524(3) the judge or justice must cancel the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order` MEANS
    IF `the judge or justice finds that the accused has contravened or had been about to contravene the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order`
        OR `the judge or justice finds that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an offence while being subject to the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall cancel a summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order in respect of the accused if the judge or justice finds that (a) ... or (b) ..."

-- 524 (4) "If the judge or justice cancels the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order, the judge or justice shall, in the following circumstances, order that the accused be detained in custody
-- unless the accused, having been given a reasonable opportunity to do so, shows cause why their detention in custody is not justified under subsection 515(10) by clearly demonstrating that their proposed release
-- plan addresses the risks posed by the accused as they relate to the grounds referred to in that subsection: (a) ... found under paragraph (3)(a) ...; (b) ... found under paragraph (3)(b) that there are
-- reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an indictable offence while being subject ...; or (c) any of the outstanding charges against the accused include charges for an offence referred
-- to in subsection 515(6) or 522(2)." "An indictable offence" is tested through s 493.01. The outstanding charges are a list of answers of s 515(6) or s 522(2) (MAYBE BOOLEAN), one for each charge.
-- src:11700 | (4) If the judge or justice cancels the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order, the judge or justice shall, in the following circumstances, order that the accused be detained in custody unless the accused, having been given a reasonable opportunity to do so, shows cause why their detention in custody is not justified under subsection 515(10) by clearly demonstrating that their proposed release plan addresses the risks posed by the accused as they relate to the grounds referred to in that subsection:
-- src:11701 | (a) the judge or justice found under paragraph (3)(a) that the accused has contravened or had been about to contravene the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order;
-- src:11702 | (b) the judge or justice found under paragraph (3)(b) that there are reasonable grounds to believe that the accused has committed an indictable offence while being subject to the summons, appearance notice, undertaking or release order; or
-- src:11703 | (c) any of the outstanding charges against the accused include charges for an offence referred to in subsection 515(6) or 522(2).
